ng the ethnicity deaths  amongst those communities in those two  nations and what it also suggests is  that there are some important factors  that are playing a role amongst the  disproportionate number of deaths and  those include socio-economic issues and  deprivation amongst the B AME community  and also underlying health conditions  now those could include things like type  2 diabetes it could include heart  problems as well which is obviously a  huge problem when it comes down to  dealing with coronavirus when you are in  intensive care and in those critical  units and also the social deprivation  comes down to a number of factors  including overcrowded housing and  cultural factors like multi-generational  housing where it really makes it quite  difficult to self isolate into social  distance in those areas meaning that  viral load is quite high now there is a  bit of discrepancy within these ons  figures and and there's a segment really  where they haven't accounted for a  specific factor and that potentially  could be a result of occupation Adam we  know that people from a black Asian  minority ethnic community are working in  these key worker roles throughout this  pandemic a lot of them on the frontline  in the NHS fighting kovat 19 a lot of  them driving buses they're more  vulnerable to getting the virus tube  drivers here in London as well and those  working in shops as well and that  potentially also has a huge role to play  in the disproportionate number of deaths  I just want to give you some more  insight into this report from the Office  of National Statistics as well they say  that males in the Bangladeshi and  Pakistani ethnic group were at one point  eight times more likely to have Co vyd  19 related deaths and women from those  backgrounds a 1.6 times more likely to  have kovat 19 related deaths to their  white counterparts and they also say  that Chinese and myth mixed ethnic  groups are also among  that group as well so clearly the ONS  stats